  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4768",
      "problem": "Given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238673/assets/aea6bc2396725e1f24bbd196f15c590fbd9628dd15f220bc57653bae25840773.svg) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238673/observations/lesson-step-17361-c7855bbec1828fc81a2d.html",
